    "media:imagetext": "- 2 -\nthat mountainless end of the island and on to Hongkong where we landed early\nin the afternoon.\nWe were met by E. M. Allison, Ki Chun and Colonel C. I.\nLiu and Jerry O'Donnell, PAA #1. The latter had secured a double room for\nus in the Gloucester Hotel - one room being equipped with an air-conditioning\nmachine - very welcome 'cause Hongkong is about 90 in both temperature and\nhumidity.\nThat night we had Chinese chow - Szechuan style - with Max Polin.\nI had forgot just how good it was !\nThe next day - Tuesday May 31 - we flew over to Canton with Colonel\nIt\nwes \u0026 perfectly useless trip because he is of no importance and doesn't know\nrequired by protocol. Arriving early at the airport me learned that the night\nbefore the whole eabinet of Ho Ying-Chin had resigned - so our visit was\neven less important. However, we went through with it and were rewarded by\na nice luncheon party in a bamboo grove on the outskirts of Canton not far\nfrom the large rambling old residence which serves as the temporary head-\nquarters for the Ministry. They are getting ready to move all the smell-fry\nclerks to Chungking while the big shots will go to Formosa.\nBy a coincidence\nonly, of course, all the monetary funds - some 250 million US dollars - are\non Formoss! The new premier - old General Yen Shi-shan - is still forming\nhis cabinet and may or may not reappoint our present Minister of Conmunications.\nThe latter - 1ike all other Chinese officials - would rather not serve.\nThey d\nall rather be free to make deels with the \"liberation\" authorities. Old Gen-\neral Yen is the same war lord who ran Shensi Province from 1911 until the Com-\nmunists captured his capital of Tai Yuan-fu some months ago. When the Japs\nthreatened Tai Yuan-fu he drove all around the city with a big sign which\nannounced that he would be is stinking furtle\" - about as complimentary as\ncalling a Frenchman a pig - if he surrendered to the Japanese. The next day\nhe left with 20 truckloads of personel effects. He did put up \u0026 great battle\nagainst the Communists and announced that he would die with his troops - but\nhe didn't. After lunch we drove into Shameen to see Lewis Clark, American\nMinister. Did some shopping but found nothing. Canton is just the ssme ex-\ncept that most of the rickshas are \"bike\" powered - which makes the traffic\nworse.\nReturning to Hongkong. It also re ains about the same - outwardly.\nHowever, all is changed.\nThe old taipans are gone.\nThe Chinese have taken\nThey have invaded the Peak where formerly none but the first families\nof British were permitted to live.\nThey have taken over the best hotels.\nThe Hongkong Hotel at \"elevens\" is 90% Chinese.\nStayed in Hongkong until Thursday then flew over to Manila to see\nthe new American Ambassador Mr. Marvin Cowan, nee Cohen. Spent Friday in\nManile.\nYou would never recognize the place.\nBoth wings of the magnificent\nGovernment building - where the legislature sat - were shot to pieces and\nhave been torn down.\nThe old intramures is a mass of ruins with only an arch\nor two remaining of the magnificent old churches. MacArthur's old headquarters\nin the corner of the wall are not only gone but the wall too is no more.\nstayed at the Manila Hotel which has been largely restored except that the fur-\nniture is \"retty\" and the whole place has a \"dow-et-the-heels\" atmosphere.\nFriday night I went out on to the dance floor about 11:30 to find only one\nAmerican (in shirt sleeves) and perhaps 100 Filipinos.",
